    {
      "id": "2103541577083719888",
      "url": "https://x.com/AnthropicAI/status/2103541577083719888",
      "author_handle": "AnthropicAI",
      "author_name": "Anthropic",
      "post_text": "New on the Science Blog: Yes, Claude can do Nine Loops.\n\nTheoretical physicists predict how particles behave using formulas called scattering amplitudes. These are notoriously hard to compute, so researchers work with layers of increasingly fine corrections called “loops”—each added loop makes the answer more precise but takes exponentially more computation. Most calculations stop at two or three loops. Eight loops was the previous record in a simplified model physicists use as a testing ground (planar N=4 super-Yang-Mills), set by SLAC's Lance Dixon and collaborators.\n\nLast month, physicist and science writer @4gravitons issued a challenge: could an AI push past eight loops in this model, using only the compute budget an academic could reasonably access?\n\nGiven a single prompt describing the nine-loop problem, Claude ran largely unsupervised for days in Claude Science and solved it using methods developed by Dixon and his colleagues, at a total cost of a few thousand dollars. Dixon independently verified the result, and von Hippel wrote about the experience for our blog.\n\nRead more:",
      "posted_at": "2026-09-25T17:46:08Z",
      "engagement": {
        "likes": 4837,
